<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nineveh</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nineveh was an ancient city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a> opposite modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a>, and the last great capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashurbanipal">Ashurbanipal</a> assembled his famous library of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> tablets there. Its capture and destruction in 612 BCE by a coalition of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/median-empire">Median Empire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonians">Babylonians</a> led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabopolassar">Nabopolassar</a> marked the fall of Assyria.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nineveh.jpg" alt="Nineveh" width="320" height="219" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Omar Siddeeq Yousif · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nineveh_-_Mashki_Gate.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The best-known example is the naturalistic sculpted portraiture of the copper-alloy head from <b>Nineveh</b> known as the Mask of Sargon, which may represent Sargon or Naram-Sin.</blockquote><a href="/empire/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Library of Ashurbanipal at <b>Nineveh</b> preserved the standard version of the Epic of Gilgamesh, one of the oldest surviving works of literature.</blockquote><a href="/empire/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The turning point was the alliance with Cyaxares of Media, which produced the sack of <b>Nineveh</b> in 612 BCE and, after the fall of Harran in 610–609 BCE, the destruction of Assyrian power.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chaldean-empire">Chaldean Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Humban-haltash III, the last significant king of the period, was later captured and paraded as a prisoner at <b>Nineveh</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/elamite-confederation">Elamite Confederation · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The siege of Lachish, depicted on reliefs in Sennacherib’s palace at <b>Nineveh</b>, left a siege ramp still visible today.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-judah">Kingdom of Judah · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Cyaxares (r. c. 625–585 BCE) restored Median power and reorganised the army, and in alliance with Nabopolassar of Babylon he destroyed Assyria, taking Ashur in 614 BCE and <b>Nineveh</b> in 612 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/median-empire">Median Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
